WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.428 --> 00:00:03.286
- [Mark] We're rolling, we're on.

00:00:03.286 --> 00:00:04.119
- Let's do it.

00:00:04.119 --> 00:00:04.952
- [Mark] We're on with Garyvee.

00:00:04.952 --> 00:00:05.785
- Thank you.

00:00:05.785 --> 00:00:06.618
- This is incredible.

00:00:06.618 --> 00:00:08.728
This is really awesome
for me to be here today.

00:00:08.728 --> 00:00:10.449
I got my man, Jim McDee who's been with me

00:00:10.449 --> 00:00:11.500
from the very beginning.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.127
He's kind of seen all the stuff
I have grow over the years.

00:00:15.127 --> 00:00:18.205
- He was a bouncer driving
a beat up Chrysler 300

00:00:18.205 --> 00:00:19.287
when I met him.

00:00:19.287 --> 00:00:20.120
- I love it.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.112
- Pretty much.

00:00:21.112 --> 00:00:22.529
- Where was that?

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.754
- Well you guys were living
in Davis at the time.

00:00:25.754 --> 00:00:26.730
- [Gary] Davis?

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:27.563
- California.

00:00:27.563 --> 00:00:30.300
- That was back in 2005,
2006 somewhere in there.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.133
- 2005.

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:31.980
- [Garyvee] You read Crush It.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.575
- Read a book called Crush It.

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:35.348
- How'd you'd you get, do you know?

00:00:35.348 --> 00:00:36.384
- My dad was into it.

00:00:36.384 --> 00:00:39.586
My dad's a huge mentor
to me much like your dad

00:00:39.586 --> 00:00:40.419
was to you.

00:00:40.419 --> 00:00:42.565
And, he's the one who
kinda told me about it.

00:00:42.565 --> 00:00:44.594
I was like, "Dad, I'm
not really into reading."

00:00:44.594 --> 00:00:45.644
I hate to read.

00:00:45.644 --> 00:00:46.477
- [Gary] Yeah me too.

00:00:46.477 --> 00:00:47.443
- It's a huge challenge for me.

00:00:47.443 --> 00:00:50.526
So I get the audio book, and it's you--

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:51.403
- Which was even better.

00:00:51.403 --> 00:00:52.958
- [Mark] Narrating it.

00:00:52.958 --> 00:00:55.756
And, I go, "Oh my God, this
guy's annoying as hell.

00:00:55.756 --> 00:00:57.297
- That's right, yep.

00:00:57.297 --> 00:00:58.348
- [Mark] This guy's got a lot of fire.

00:00:58.348 --> 00:00:59.748
But, once I started listening to it more--

00:00:59.748 --> 00:01:01.690
- You were able to get
through that, right?

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.036
- [Mark] I was able to get
through that just fine.

00:01:03.036 --> 00:01:04.502
- Cause some people don't

00:01:04.502 --> 00:01:05.918
- Well the reason I'm
able to get through it

00:01:05.918 --> 00:01:09.279
is because I realized
very quickly into the book

00:01:09.279 --> 00:01:10.591
that that's who you are.

00:01:10.591 --> 00:01:13.420
So when somebody's trying to
be amplified or over the top--

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:14.612
- [Garyvee] Sure if
they're forcing it yet.

00:01:14.612 --> 00:01:16.059
- For bullshit reasons,
then I'm like fuck this guy.

00:01:16.059 --> 00:01:16.933
- I get it.

00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:18.611
I'll take anybody in their pure for.

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:19.519
Anybody.

00:01:19.519 --> 00:01:20.725
- [Mark] I enjoyed it a lot.

00:01:20.725 --> 00:01:22.323
Some the of the general
principles you shared

00:01:22.323 --> 00:01:25.035
in that book helped me
and my wife tremendously.

00:01:25.035 --> 00:01:27.030
- [Jim] Experience when my
best friend passed away.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.780
He was a colonel at the Pentagon.

00:01:30.743 --> 00:01:32.194
He had a ton of people at his funeral.

00:01:32.194 --> 00:01:34.083
Everybody was saying these
fantastic things about him.

00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:37.165
Like I knew he was a great
guy, but there were people

00:01:37.165 --> 00:01:39.684
saying that he changed
the course of their lives.

00:01:39.684 --> 00:01:42.102
I'm like, "Have I done that for anybody?

00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:42.935
- [Garyvee] Yeah I get it.

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:45.723
- [Jim] Like how many
pallbearers can I pull together?

00:01:45.723 --> 00:01:46.802
- I get it.

00:01:46.802 --> 00:01:48.522
- [Mark] Jordan has told me that fitness

00:01:48.522 --> 00:01:50.183
has changed your life, and
it's put a lot of money

00:01:50.183 --> 00:01:51.914
in your bank account.

00:01:51.914 --> 00:01:52.902
- Is that true?

00:01:52.902 --> 00:01:54.177
(laughing)

00:01:54.177 --> 00:01:57.293
- [Jordan] I did not say that.

00:01:57.293 --> 00:02:00.330
- He actually said almost
kind of the exact opposite.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.216
- You know it's
interesting a lot of people

00:02:02.216 --> 00:02:03.744
ask me about getting into better shape

00:02:03.744 --> 00:02:04.917
over the last couple of years.

00:02:04.917 --> 00:02:07.629
You know it's so funny
it's the same way I think

00:02:07.629 --> 00:02:09.343
about business and life.

00:02:09.343 --> 00:02:13.188
It's unbelievable to me that
I don't necessarily feel

00:02:13.188 --> 00:02:14.021
so much better.

00:02:14.021 --> 00:02:16.117
Like we keep, you know,
and you guys probably are

00:02:16.117 --> 00:02:17.043
more grounded than this.

00:02:17.043 --> 00:02:19.760
It's been fun to realize
you keep finding new spots.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.613
Like oh shit, now I've
realized my shoulder.

00:02:21.613 --> 00:02:24.403
This is literally last week.

00:02:24.403 --> 00:02:25.952
You don't know it.

00:02:25.952 --> 00:02:28.548
I didn't know at three,
you know, I knew that I was

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:30.574
not doing anything that was smart

00:02:30.574 --> 00:02:33.268
with my eating or physical
fitness three years ago

00:02:33.268 --> 00:02:34.836
which is why I started doing it.

00:02:34.836 --> 00:02:36.700
- [Mark] You kind of
stuffed it under the table.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.010
- You know I did it because I knew

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:39.705
I wasn't doing the right behavior.

00:02:39.705 --> 00:02:43.674
But, to say I feel so
different, there's a couple

00:02:43.674 --> 00:02:44.507
little things.

00:02:44.507 --> 00:02:48.215
Like I know when I carry Xander
down the street one armed

00:02:48.215 --> 00:02:50.953
for four blocks and don't
get tired after three steps.

00:02:50.953 --> 00:02:52.137
That makes sense.

00:02:52.137 --> 00:02:53.559
Grabbing my luggage.

00:02:53.559 --> 00:02:55.748
But, energy-wise I
don't feel any different

00:02:55.748 --> 00:02:58.110
because I have so much natural energy.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.297
- [Mark] You had a lot of energy anyway.

00:02:59.297 --> 00:03:01.503
- But, even though I don't feel

00:03:01.503 --> 00:03:04.503
these remarkable short term results,

00:03:06.141 --> 00:03:08.502
I know how this plays out.

00:03:08.502 --> 00:03:10.971
I know what my life looks like at 74.

00:03:10.971 --> 00:03:12.698
You know you gotta
understand supply and demand.

00:03:12.698 --> 00:03:13.776
- [Jordan] The pond got bigger.

00:03:13.776 --> 00:03:16.741
- [Mark] What we've seen in
our community is people's lifts

00:03:16.741 --> 00:03:17.916
getting a lot better.

00:03:17.916 --> 00:03:20.593
So I'm watching him, I watching this guy,

00:03:20.593 --> 00:03:22.568
I'm watching that guy,
and because the lifts

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:26.541
have gotten so much bigger,
now what a 700 pound

00:03:26.541 --> 00:03:28.732
deadlift used to be kind of the norm.

00:03:28.732 --> 00:03:30.518
- The norm is like exceptional.

00:03:30.518 --> 00:03:33.494
- Well now we're seeing,
yeah in power lifting.

00:03:33.494 --> 00:03:37.309
Now we're seeing 800, 900,
1,000 pound deadlifts.

00:03:37.309 --> 00:03:40.692
What do you think Instagram
has done for business?

00:03:40.692 --> 00:03:42.315
Has it done something
similar where they're seeing

00:03:42.315 --> 00:03:44.994
somebody like you, and it's
driving them to push themselves?

00:03:44.994 --> 00:03:46.903
- That's a really good observation.

00:03:46.903 --> 00:03:51.070
So I think what that does
when you have more awareness

00:03:52.168 --> 00:03:55.879
of everything, what it
does is exposes people.

00:03:55.879 --> 00:03:58.467
So I don't think there's
a general answer of that.

00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:01.717
Winners say, "Fuck 750, I'm going 760."

00:04:04.206 --> 00:04:06.642
A lot of people go the other way

00:04:06.642 --> 00:04:09.388
and get discouraged and bow out.

00:04:09.388 --> 00:04:11.357
It's the way I think about rich kids.

00:04:11.357 --> 00:04:13.274
I've now, I've succumb.

00:04:14.337 --> 00:04:15.744
Something I tried, I
don't know how I thought

00:04:15.744 --> 00:04:16.775
I was gonna fix this.

00:04:16.775 --> 00:04:18.126
But, like I'm aware that my children

00:04:18.126 --> 00:04:21.666
are gonna grow up extremely well off.

00:04:21.666 --> 00:04:23.252
And, they're gonna do one or two things

00:04:23.252 --> 00:04:25.001
once they realize what I'm up to.

00:04:25.001 --> 00:04:27.031
They're gonna look at
that, and they're gonna say

00:04:27.031 --> 00:04:29.657
what I did with my dad
which from where we were

00:04:29.657 --> 00:04:31.160
seemed like a big accomplishment,

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.298
"I'm gonna climb that mountain."

00:04:32.298 --> 00:04:33.589
I mean I used to tell my dad like,

00:04:33.589 --> 00:04:35.884
"I'm gonna do so much more than you

00:04:35.884 --> 00:04:38.647
"that you're gonna feel
that it's disrespectful

00:04:38.647 --> 00:04:39.749
"to even put up our names."

00:04:39.749 --> 00:04:41.202
I was so competitive with my own dad.

00:04:41.202 --> 00:04:42.741
I'm like, "You're not
even gonna say my name

00:04:42.741 --> 00:04:44.068
"because it's gonna be disrespectful

00:04:44.068 --> 00:04:45.139
"to compare the two of us."

00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:46.019
And, he would get mad too.

00:04:46.019 --> 00:04:46.892
We were competitive.

00:04:46.892 --> 00:04:50.279
You know I've been watching,
now I have fancy friends,

00:04:50.279 --> 00:04:52.677
well-off friends, third
generation wealth friends,

00:04:52.677 --> 00:04:55.184
and a lot of kids go
completely the other way.

00:04:55.184 --> 00:04:57.319
They're gonna be like, "Well
I'm not gonna climb that.

00:04:57.319 --> 00:04:59.944
"And, actually I kinda
feel guilty that I fly

00:04:59.944 --> 00:05:02.165
"in private jets and have a Hamptons home.

00:05:02.165 --> 00:05:04.303
"So what I'm actually
gonna do is I'm gonna take

00:05:04.303 --> 00:05:06.487
"mommy and daddy's money and give it away.

00:05:06.487 --> 00:05:08.554
"I'm gonna be a nonprofit,
or I'm gonna go build homes."

00:05:08.554 --> 00:05:11.038
And, by the way, I'm
comfortable with whatever Misha

00:05:11.038 --> 00:05:12.039
and Xander decide to do.

00:05:12.039 --> 00:05:12.963
I genuinely am.

00:05:12.963 --> 00:05:16.068
But, that's what happens when
you get exposed to big things.

00:05:16.068 --> 00:05:19.302
You either decide, and the
reason you see 800 and more now

00:05:19.302 --> 00:05:22.330
is a small percentage of the great winners

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.393
put in the triple work
because they're aware

00:05:25.393 --> 00:05:26.226
and push it forward.

00:05:26.226 --> 00:05:28.171
One of the biggest reasons
athletes are better

00:05:28.171 --> 00:05:30.446
in today's world is
Magic Johnson didn't know

00:05:30.446 --> 00:05:33.277
that Larry Bird was
taking 5,000 free throws.

00:05:33.277 --> 00:05:34.156
- Yeah.

00:05:34.156 --> 00:05:35.714
- They just found each other in college.

00:05:35.714 --> 00:05:37.814
And like, "Damn."

00:05:37.814 --> 00:05:39.075
- Greatest rivalry of all time.

00:05:39.075 --> 00:05:40.422
- Now, everybody's, you
know, when you're six,

00:05:40.422 --> 00:05:42.403
seven, eight, nine, 10, 11, 12 years old,

00:05:42.403 --> 00:05:44.364
they're grading kids at such a young.

00:05:44.364 --> 00:05:46.518
You know where you sit.

00:05:46.518 --> 00:05:49.963
And, it's pushing you harder
if you're really that star.

00:05:49.963 --> 00:05:53.410
And, so maybe Larry Bird
would've taken 3,000 shots

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:56.957
if he knew Magic was taking 1,200.

00:05:56.957 --> 00:05:58.413
- [Mark] How old are your kids?

00:05:58.413 --> 00:05:59.643
- Seven and four.

00:05:59.643 --> 00:06:02.563
- [Mark] What do you think
is the most important

00:06:02.563 --> 00:06:05.103
thing to do with them
in terms of, you know

00:06:05.103 --> 00:06:07.235
a parent is gonna have to be the one

00:06:07.235 --> 00:06:08.686
that has to guide their kid.

00:06:08.686 --> 00:06:10.086
You're gonna have to make your kid

00:06:10.086 --> 00:06:12.239
do things that they don't
wanna do such as like

00:06:12.239 --> 00:06:14.093
eat vegetables or finish their dinner.

00:06:14.093 --> 00:06:17.843
Just not eat too much
sugar and so on, right?

00:06:19.383 --> 00:06:20.775
- Or not.

00:06:20.775 --> 00:06:23.225
I think one of the great
debates that I play with now

00:06:23.225 --> 00:06:27.478
is what is established,
cliche eat your vegetables,

00:06:27.478 --> 00:06:31.645
and what is actually the
value of that in reality?

00:06:32.545 --> 00:06:34.528
Truth is the far majority of people I know

00:06:34.528 --> 00:06:37.398
probably didn't eat their vegetables.

00:06:37.398 --> 00:06:41.156
Definitely anybody who's
41, ate way too much sugar

00:06:41.156 --> 00:06:45.955
and drank soda, I mean I drank
soda everyday of my life.

00:06:45.955 --> 00:06:49.728
To me, the things I think
most about are number one,

00:06:49.728 --> 00:06:52.353
two, three, and four is
to build self-esteem.

00:06:52.353 --> 00:06:57.230
So my belief is that
self-esteem is the only drug

00:06:57.230 --> 00:06:59.147
that combats the world.

00:07:00.034 --> 00:07:00.867
I genuinely believe that.

00:07:00.867 --> 00:07:01.987
- [Mark] That's powerful.

00:07:01.987 --> 00:07:04.822
- In parallel, it's imperative
that you don't build

00:07:04.822 --> 00:07:06.239
fake self-esteem.

00:07:07.076 --> 00:07:10.770
Self-esteem can't be
manufactured through rewarding

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.520
non-rewarding events.

00:07:13.624 --> 00:07:15.341
- [Jim] Like participation trophies.

00:07:15.341 --> 00:07:16.174
- Correct.

00:07:16.174 --> 00:07:19.399
You can't come in eight
place in the race in school

00:07:19.399 --> 00:07:21.939
and then get prized
for that because that's

00:07:21.939 --> 00:07:23.529
just not real life.

00:07:23.529 --> 00:07:26.418
However, Xander for example
this weekend when Max

00:07:26.418 --> 00:07:30.041
got hurt, his nephew,
stopped what he was doing

00:07:30.041 --> 00:07:32.316
and came over and showed amazing empathy

00:07:32.316 --> 00:07:33.591
for a four year old.

00:07:33.591 --> 00:07:35.918
That has become now the
thing I've talked to him

00:07:35.918 --> 00:07:36.983
about for two days.

00:07:36.983 --> 00:07:40.627
That's he's incredible, that
you have such a great heart.

00:07:40.627 --> 00:07:42.162
To me, you triple down.

00:07:42.162 --> 00:07:43.633
This is by the way this is me being

00:07:43.633 --> 00:07:46.422
a byproduct of being what
I think perfectly parented.

00:07:46.422 --> 00:07:47.890
This is what my mom did.

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:52.057
My mom over exaggerated my
natural goodness as a person

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.545
to make me believe it was
the most important thing.

00:07:56.545 --> 00:08:00.552
And, she never gave me
third place trophies.

00:08:00.552 --> 00:08:02.606
And, when I lost, I lost.

00:08:02.606 --> 00:08:05.104
And, she made me realize there
were repercussions in life.

00:08:05.104 --> 00:08:06.892
So when I got bad grades
even though she knew

00:08:06.892 --> 00:08:11.598
I was gonna be very special,
I was literally punished.

00:08:11.598 --> 00:08:12.946
It was like clockwork.

00:08:12.946 --> 00:08:15.375
You know how like the sun
and the moon do their thing?

00:08:15.375 --> 00:08:18.875
From fifth grade to senior of high school,

00:08:19.767 --> 00:08:23.027
I was punished every single mid October,

00:08:23.027 --> 00:08:26.234
that's the first report
card to late December,

00:08:26.234 --> 00:08:29.363
early March and the end of the year.

00:08:29.363 --> 00:08:30.243
- [Mark] What age?

00:08:30.243 --> 00:08:33.355
- Literally from fifth grade,
for seven to eight years

00:08:33.355 --> 00:08:34.823
consistently I was grounded.

00:08:34.823 --> 00:08:35.729
- [Woman] I know, we're
in the middle of that.

00:08:35.729 --> 00:08:38.441
- You know I think it's interesting.

00:08:38.441 --> 00:08:42.032
I think that subconsciously I knew

00:08:42.032 --> 00:08:44.458
that it wasn't the most
important thing in the world,

00:08:44.458 --> 00:08:46.995
but I do like that she
punished me and didn't let me

00:08:46.995 --> 00:08:48.956
completely disrespect the system.

00:08:48.956 --> 00:08:52.373
But, she never let it hurt my confidence.

00:08:54.155 --> 00:08:55.873
She played it very smart.

00:08:55.873 --> 00:08:57.834
And, there is something
interesting my parents did.

00:08:57.834 --> 00:09:01.547
At 14 they said, "Look
tough guy, you're not gonna

00:09:01.547 --> 00:09:04.224
"be a student, it means you're gonna work.

00:09:04.224 --> 00:09:06.867
"And, so that means you're
gonna start working now."

00:09:06.867 --> 00:09:09.893
So I do think one of the
great things my parents did,

00:09:09.893 --> 00:09:11.248
and I think a lot of parents
should think about this

00:09:11.248 --> 00:09:14.418
is if you don't have a student,
even if they're an artist

00:09:14.418 --> 00:09:18.039
and wanna sing and paint,
you need to figure out

00:09:18.039 --> 00:09:20.295
how to establish massive work ethic.

00:09:20.295 --> 00:09:22.781
Because the only thing that's controllable

00:09:22.781 --> 00:09:25.585
for somebody who's gonna
be on themselves in life

00:09:25.585 --> 00:09:26.859
is their work ethic.

00:09:26.859 --> 00:09:28.588
Their talent, you can get it better.

00:09:28.588 --> 00:09:30.688
Right you can lift more, you
can get better at things,

00:09:30.688 --> 00:09:35.661
but like my calves and ass
aren't naturally the same

00:09:35.661 --> 00:09:36.540
as everybody else.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:37.876
And, there's people
who are naturally like,

00:09:37.876 --> 00:09:39.779
I don't like my chest is never gonna be

00:09:39.779 --> 00:09:41.548
as big as I necessarily
want it 'casue there's

00:09:41.548 --> 00:09:43.123
certain things that are, right?

00:09:43.123 --> 00:09:45.310
But, meanwhile like I'll sell you back

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:47.552
the hat you're wearing right now.

00:09:47.552 --> 00:09:48.425
I will.

00:09:48.425 --> 00:09:49.309
(laughing)

00:09:49.309 --> 00:09:53.586
So I think it's important
to teach work ethic

00:09:53.586 --> 00:09:56.683
because work ethic is the only thing

00:09:56.683 --> 00:09:59.153
that maximizes what you naturally have.

00:09:59.153 --> 00:10:01.429
And, it's really the best you can ask for.

00:10:01.429 --> 00:10:02.774
- [Mark] What about sports?

00:10:02.774 --> 00:10:04.139
What about sports with your children?

00:10:04.139 --> 00:10:05.472
- I'm very into it.

00:10:05.472 --> 00:10:07.534
I'm a little worried about
what the New York City

00:10:07.534 --> 00:10:08.987
dynamic of sports is.

00:10:08.987 --> 00:10:09.911
I didn't grow up--

00:10:09.911 --> 00:10:11.506
- Sports are confusing
today it seems like.

00:10:11.506 --> 00:10:14.972
You have to be a part of
something for so long.

00:10:14.972 --> 00:10:16.037
- Yeah sports are tricky.

00:10:16.037 --> 00:10:16.875
- It's a different commitment.

00:10:16.875 --> 00:10:18.217
- Here's what I would say.

00:10:18.217 --> 00:10:19.792
I'm gonna use sports a
little bit differently.

00:10:19.792 --> 00:10:21.807
I'm obsessed with competition.

00:10:21.807 --> 00:10:24.307
And, I really hope my
kids are competitive.

00:10:24.307 --> 00:10:26.288
One, I'll say it again, I'm not interested

00:10:26.288 --> 00:10:28.528
imposing any of my will on them.

00:10:28.528 --> 00:10:32.221
If they're not competitive,
it'll be disappointing

00:10:32.221 --> 00:10:34.425
'cause I love it for about four seconds,

00:10:34.425 --> 00:10:36.956
and then I'll trick myself
into thinking it's great.

00:10:36.956 --> 00:10:40.389
Like I'm just gonna go optimistic on it.

00:10:40.389 --> 00:10:41.267
Sports is cool.

00:10:41.267 --> 00:10:42.282
Team sports are cool.

00:10:42.282 --> 00:10:43.995
I think it teaches people a lot of things.

00:10:43.995 --> 00:10:45.712
- [Mark] The messages that
they might learn there--

00:10:45.712 --> 00:10:47.286
- I mean listen I love sports.

00:10:47.286 --> 00:10:48.211
I love sports.

00:10:48.211 --> 00:10:49.435
I love sports.

00:10:49.435 --> 00:10:51.223
My kids have shown interest
is sports this year

00:10:51.223 --> 00:10:52.640
for the first time in a meaningful way.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:55.683
My daughter especially which
is a lot of fun for me.

00:10:55.683 --> 00:10:58.377
Yeah, again I'm very weird.

00:10:58.377 --> 00:11:02.294
Like I really want to
reverse engineer my kids.

00:11:03.926 --> 00:11:06.446
I wanna pay attention to their reactions,

00:11:06.446 --> 00:11:08.005
figure out what's driving them,

00:11:08.005 --> 00:11:12.206
and then quadruple down
on it, and then instill

00:11:12.206 --> 00:11:15.091
the sprit of reality into it.

00:11:15.091 --> 00:11:16.315
- [Mark] How do you pay attention to them

00:11:16.315 --> 00:11:18.330
with so many different things going on?

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:19.205
- That's hard.

00:11:19.205 --> 00:11:20.450
And, I would tell you that--

00:11:20.450 --> 00:11:21.846
[Mark] Like sometimes
you're on your phone,

00:11:21.846 --> 00:11:24.683
and you just fucking throw
it over your shoulder.

00:11:24.683 --> 00:11:26.748
- I think my answer is I try.

00:11:26.748 --> 00:11:28.849
First of all, I work too much.

00:11:28.849 --> 00:11:30.512
So the limited time I have with them,

00:11:30.512 --> 00:11:32.259
I try to stay focused.

00:11:32.259 --> 00:11:35.114
But, like I'm selfish in my own stuff too.

00:11:35.114 --> 00:11:36.932
I think it's important to be.

00:11:36.932 --> 00:11:38.405
You know I think one of
the things I worry about

00:11:38.405 --> 00:11:39.893
with a lot of stay at home parents.

00:11:39.893 --> 00:11:42.145
And, obviously I don't know
what the percentage are,

00:11:42.145 --> 00:11:44.302
but I think we still live in a world

00:11:44.302 --> 00:11:46.806
where it's gotta be in
the 80s maybe even 90s

00:11:46.806 --> 00:11:47.888
that the woman's staying at home.

00:11:47.888 --> 00:11:49.392
I watch all these, you know
there's incredible women

00:11:49.392 --> 00:11:51.561
that work for me, women entrepreneurs.

00:11:51.561 --> 00:11:53.450
You know it's been interesting for me.

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:55.396
It feels like such a difficult game.

00:11:55.396 --> 00:11:57.651
You know guys have it
so easy when children

00:11:57.651 --> 00:11:58.713
come into the equation.

00:11:58.713 --> 00:12:00.836
I feel like there's an enormous amount,

00:12:00.836 --> 00:12:04.253
you know the balance of guilt, of keeping

00:12:05.245 --> 00:12:08.995
your own identity, of
so many of these women.

00:12:10.269 --> 00:12:13.429
We're living in a great age
where it's not the archaic

00:12:13.429 --> 00:12:14.302
ages anymore.

00:12:14.302 --> 00:12:16.407
There's a lot of women that
have unbelievable ambitions,

00:12:16.407 --> 00:12:18.720
the greatest ambitions equal
to the ambitions of men

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:22.145
which they didn't have
50, 60, 30, 90 years ago.

00:12:22.145 --> 00:12:24.495
And, so like that transition
seems very difficult.

00:12:24.495 --> 00:12:28.600
And, I think when you lose
yourself in your children,

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:29.549
you lose.

00:12:29.549 --> 00:12:32.170
You lose which then
means your children lose.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:36.735
One of the things that I'm
very, very obsessed with is

00:12:36.735 --> 00:12:38.351
I can't believe how many parents wrap

00:12:38.351 --> 00:12:41.714
their own self-esteem into their
children's accomplishments.

00:12:41.714 --> 00:12:43.167
- [Mark] That's wild isn't it?

00:12:43.167 --> 00:12:46.717
- So this has been the
big eye opener for me.

00:12:46.717 --> 00:12:48.119
This was probably the one thing I didn't,

00:12:48.119 --> 00:12:51.589
I think I anticipated quite
a bit about parenting.

00:12:51.589 --> 00:12:52.563
I think I nailed it.

00:12:52.563 --> 00:12:53.666
I'm good at predicting shit.

00:12:53.666 --> 00:12:54.555
(laughing)

00:12:54.555 --> 00:12:56.937
Here's where I missed by a mile.

00:12:56.937 --> 00:13:01.284
Like literally it struck
me at a parent event.

00:13:01.284 --> 00:13:04.617
I was like wait a minute, wait a minute,

00:13:06.476 --> 00:13:09.726
you actually feel better about yourself

00:13:10.837 --> 00:13:13.572
'cause your kid can play chess.

00:13:13.572 --> 00:13:17.173
You, not you love your
kid and you're happy

00:13:17.173 --> 00:13:18.006
for your kid.

00:13:18.006 --> 00:13:20.863
No, you think you're better
'cause you're the parent

00:13:20.863 --> 00:13:22.654
of the chess player.

00:13:22.654 --> 00:13:24.106
- [Mark] You think you're a bad ass.

00:13:24.106 --> 00:13:25.400
- It blew me away.

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:27.690
My parents never wrapped
their self-esteem in me.

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:31.857
I wrap my self-esteem in only
myself not even my parents.

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:37.572
I'm very empathetic which
is why I'm a good salesman

00:13:37.572 --> 00:13:39.037
and why I'm successful.

00:13:39.037 --> 00:13:42.116
That's my gift, gratitude and empathy.

00:13:42.116 --> 00:13:44.988
I wasn't empathetic to that
'casue I haven't lived it.

00:13:44.988 --> 00:13:46.054
I never tasted it.

00:13:46.054 --> 00:13:47.174
I never understood it.

00:13:47.174 --> 00:13:48.436
And, then it opened up everything.

00:13:48.436 --> 00:13:51.078
Like that's why people put bumper stickers

00:13:51.078 --> 00:13:52.411
of like Harvard.

00:13:53.891 --> 00:13:56.797
I'm like, oh shit, that's why parents

00:13:56.797 --> 00:13:59.880
force their kids into collecting debt

00:14:01.226 --> 00:14:03.801
to go to a university that
makes the parent feel good

00:14:03.801 --> 00:14:05.600
verus the kid going to a secondary school

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:08.053
or a community college where they don't.

00:14:08.053 --> 00:14:12.512
It unlocked a whole world
of like fuck you parents.

00:14:12.512 --> 00:14:14.422
(laughing)

00:14:14.422 --> 00:14:18.217
- I saw that in Little
League with my older son.

00:14:18.217 --> 00:14:21.503
Two of the assistant coaches for his team,

00:14:21.503 --> 00:14:23.515
they would have to call the sheriff

00:14:23.515 --> 00:14:25.409
to calm them down because
if there was a call

00:14:25.409 --> 00:14:27.309
at home plate that they
didn't like or a strike,

00:14:27.309 --> 00:14:28.184
or whatever--

00:14:28.184 --> 00:14:29.058
- That I actually agree with.

00:14:29.058 --> 00:14:30.024
I want a fight.

00:14:30.024 --> 00:14:30.857
I get sports.

00:14:30.857 --> 00:14:33.940
That to me could be a nuance of that.

00:14:36.224 --> 00:14:39.294
That could be true if Rick wants to fight

00:14:39.294 --> 00:14:41.126
because he thinks he's little Timmy,

00:14:41.126 --> 00:14:43.015
and he's a great baseball player.

00:14:43.015 --> 00:14:45.589
I would just fight because I just want

00:14:45.589 --> 00:14:46.814
my kid's team to win the game.

00:14:46.814 --> 00:14:48.617
Yeah I just want to win the game.

00:14:48.617 --> 00:14:50.487
- [Mark] It does get pretty heated.

00:14:50.487 --> 00:14:53.605
- To me, those things actually
don't really freak me out

00:14:53.605 --> 00:14:55.840
as much as the politically
correct world gets freaked out.

00:14:55.840 --> 00:15:00.007
I just don't think it makes
any sense in the world.

00:15:01.286 --> 00:15:02.169
You're a parent.

00:15:02.169 --> 00:15:03.753
You're number one thing in the world

00:15:03.753 --> 00:15:06.496
is to do everything right by that child.

00:15:06.496 --> 00:15:07.568
Like they're at your mercy.

00:15:07.568 --> 00:15:09.755
They're your kid.

00:15:09.755 --> 00:15:12.005
And, parents are misfiring.

00:15:15.019 --> 00:15:16.919
They're selfish.

00:15:16.919 --> 00:15:18.657
- You ever heard this quote before?

00:15:18.657 --> 00:15:20.688
The best way to have
an impact on the world

00:15:20.688 --> 00:15:22.455
is to go home and love your family,

00:15:22.455 --> 00:15:23.995
you ever heard that before.

00:15:23.995 --> 00:15:25.215
It's by Mother Teresa.

00:15:25.215 --> 00:15:27.068
- [Garyvee] Is that right?

00:15:27.068 --> 00:15:28.002
- I think it's great.

00:15:28.002 --> 00:15:29.160
It makes a lot of sense to me

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:31.553
because I'm trying to impact,
you know lifters like this.

00:15:31.553 --> 00:15:33.493
I'm trying to impact trainers and people

00:15:33.493 --> 00:15:34.458
in the fitness industry.

00:15:34.458 --> 00:15:35.765
And, I'm super excited about it.

00:15:35.765 --> 00:15:37.958
Then I'm at home, and I'm in my phone.

00:15:37.958 --> 00:15:38.791
- I get it man.

00:15:38.791 --> 00:15:40.040
- [Mark] And, my little girl's saying,

00:15:40.040 --> 00:15:40.873
"Daddy, daddy, daddy?"

00:15:40.873 --> 00:15:42.333
And, I'm like why am I doing this?

00:15:42.333 --> 00:15:45.876
- I get it, and I think that's right.

00:15:45.876 --> 00:15:48.566
I think it comes down to
your circumstance too.

00:15:48.566 --> 00:15:52.698
So I'm the son of a first
generation immigrant

00:15:52.698 --> 00:15:55.325
who worked every minute,
left before I woke up,

00:15:55.325 --> 00:15:58.021
came home after I slept, slept in the home

00:15:58.021 --> 00:16:02.430
that I slept in every single
night, took zero travel days.

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:05.903
So was in the same home, my dad slept.

00:16:05.903 --> 00:16:08.170
This is not a divorce, this
is not a traveling father

00:16:08.170 --> 00:16:09.048
like I am.

00:16:09.048 --> 00:16:11.583
This is my father slept in the same home

00:16:11.583 --> 00:16:14.524
that I slept in my entire life,

00:16:14.524 --> 00:16:16.241
I just want everybody to hear this

00:16:16.241 --> 00:16:18.777
'cause this is insane
actually in hindsight,

00:16:18.777 --> 00:16:21.341
From the day I was born
to the day I left school,

00:16:21.341 --> 00:16:22.702
like all of that.

00:16:22.702 --> 00:16:26.001
Like old school, like just
came home every night.

00:16:26.001 --> 00:16:27.491
I don't know what else to tell you.

00:16:27.491 --> 00:16:28.872
And, I never saw him once.

00:16:28.872 --> 00:16:30.818
And, when I tell you I never saw him once,

00:16:30.818 --> 00:16:34.283
I spent more time with my kids
this Presidents' Day weekend

00:16:34.283 --> 00:16:39.223
than my dad did with me my
entire childhood combined.

00:16:39.223 --> 00:16:40.457
I don't think people--

00:16:40.457 --> 00:16:43.434
[Mark] Was there ever a
term of being resentful?

00:16:43.434 --> 00:16:45.355
- Didn't even cross my mind.

00:16:45.355 --> 00:16:46.960
- [Jim] What was your impression of it?

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:48.362
- Thank God dad works so hard.

00:16:48.362 --> 00:16:50.688
We came from nothing,
now we have a townhouse.

00:16:50.688 --> 00:16:52.171
That's what it was.

00:16:52.171 --> 00:16:53.329
It was gratitude.

00:16:53.329 --> 00:16:54.452
It was optimism.

00:16:54.452 --> 00:16:55.569
And, there wasn't a need.

00:16:55.569 --> 00:16:56.636
My mom was so incredible.

00:16:56.636 --> 00:16:58.576
And, again every kid's different.

00:16:58.576 --> 00:17:00.136
I didn't need my dad.

00:17:00.136 --> 00:17:03.300
Did I notice that a lot of people's dads

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:05.032
were at the Little League games?

00:17:05.032 --> 00:17:05.906
Absolutely.

00:17:05.906 --> 00:17:08.146
My dad went to one Little League game

00:17:08.146 --> 00:17:09.792
my entire career, and I was pumped.

00:17:09.792 --> 00:17:10.858
And, I did good.

00:17:10.858 --> 00:17:11.960
It was an all star game.

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:12.793
I played well.

00:17:12.793 --> 00:17:16.583
But, again I was a kid
that didn't need it.

00:17:16.583 --> 00:17:17.505
I didn't need my dad.

00:17:17.505 --> 00:17:18.995
I definitely needed my mom.

00:17:18.995 --> 00:17:21.342
You need something, but
like I didn't need anything.

00:17:21.342 --> 00:17:23.922
I've never been somebody
who validates himself

00:17:23.922 --> 00:17:26.330
on anything on the outside.

00:17:26.330 --> 00:17:27.417
And, that is a gift.

00:17:27.417 --> 00:17:28.570
And, I think that goes back.

00:17:28.570 --> 00:17:29.677
You have one?

00:17:29.677 --> 00:17:30.586
- [Mark] I have two kids.

00:17:30.586 --> 00:17:31.465
- Two.

00:17:31.465 --> 00:17:32.654
Look they're gonna be different.

00:17:32.654 --> 00:17:34.660
They're different.

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:37.440
Like Misha, my daughter, reminds me of me

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:39.157
because she never gave a shit

00:17:39.157 --> 00:17:40.752
that I would leave the house.

00:17:40.752 --> 00:17:41.625
"Bye dad."

00:17:41.625 --> 00:17:43.566
"Misha, I'm gonna be gone for four days.

00:17:43.566 --> 00:17:44.444
"I'll be back."

00:17:44.444 --> 00:17:45.318
"Bye, dad."

00:17:45.318 --> 00:17:46.750
My little guy's like not.

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:48.726
And, I'm like oh fuck I didn't realize

00:17:48.726 --> 00:17:49.859
how good I had it with Misha.

00:17:49.859 --> 00:17:51.388
Like this actually hurts my feelings.

00:17:51.388 --> 00:17:52.221
I'm emotional.

00:17:52.221 --> 00:17:53.054
This is sad.

00:17:53.054 --> 00:17:54.025
And, you'll adjust.

00:17:54.025 --> 00:17:56.269
The other thing is I just
said to Tyler, my admin,

00:17:56.269 --> 00:17:58.793
like, and this is where
I was going with this,

00:17:58.793 --> 00:18:00.333
this if very long winded to the thing,

00:18:00.333 --> 00:18:01.841
how old are your kids?

00:18:01.841 --> 00:18:03.187
- [Woman] 13 and nine.

00:18:03.187 --> 00:18:06.523
- Right so you guys are
really in this zone,

00:18:06.523 --> 00:18:10.617
you know how you said
the vegetables thing,

00:18:10.617 --> 00:18:13.108
I'm very against that.

00:18:13.108 --> 00:18:14.277
- [Mark] I'm against vegetables too.

00:18:14.277 --> 00:18:15.451
(laughing)

00:18:15.451 --> 00:18:17.238
Let's make a fucking stand together.

00:18:17.238 --> 00:18:18.569
- Here's a huge irony.

00:18:18.569 --> 00:18:19.462
You know this.

00:18:19.462 --> 00:18:20.336
I love vegetables.

00:18:20.336 --> 00:18:21.647
I'm against the cliche thing.

00:18:21.647 --> 00:18:23.203
So one of the biggest
conversations I'm having

00:18:23.203 --> 00:18:24.781
with my wife right now
is like, "Look Lizzie,

00:18:24.781 --> 00:18:28.328
"I got to Oslo, Hong Kong, Melbourne.

00:18:28.328 --> 00:18:29.525
"I'm going on all these amazing trips.

00:18:29.525 --> 00:18:32.745
"I know that you're a little
bit more by the book,"

00:18:32.745 --> 00:18:35.371
that's my wife, "Like
I need you to be open

00:18:35.371 --> 00:18:38.348
"to what I wanna do which
is Misha's gonna miss

00:18:38.348 --> 00:18:41.375
"13 days of school a
year that make no sense."

00:18:41.375 --> 00:18:44.903
And, that's because I'm
gonna take her to Oslo.

00:18:44.903 --> 00:18:46.901
And, she's gonna see what daddy does,

00:18:46.901 --> 00:18:49.284
and we're gonna spend
six hours, eight hours

00:18:49.284 --> 00:18:51.034
on the plan together.

00:18:51.899 --> 00:18:54.316
We'll change, and you change.

00:18:55.159 --> 00:18:57.309
When I sleep in one place twice,

00:18:57.309 --> 00:18:59.077
we slept in Belgium
two nights, it's weird.

00:18:59.077 --> 00:19:02.744
I stay nowhere more
than one night, nowhere.

00:19:03.623 --> 00:19:05.218
Trips are to Europe and Asia.

00:19:05.218 --> 00:19:07.351
These are eight hour
trips, nine hour trips,

00:19:07.351 --> 00:19:08.363
13 hour trips.

00:19:08.363 --> 00:19:09.576
They are insane.

00:19:09.576 --> 00:19:10.607
- [Ryan] We didn't even
stay a night in Ireland.

00:19:10.607 --> 00:19:11.440
We left.

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:14.081
- Right, I went to Dublin,
never even slept there.

00:19:14.081 --> 00:19:16.621
Went from New York overnight to Dublin.

00:19:16.621 --> 00:19:19.663
Dublin, speech, get on
a plane go to Belgium.

00:19:19.663 --> 00:19:22.188
That's why we had two nights in Belgium.

00:19:22.188 --> 00:19:23.168
- [Woman] Like a rockstar.

00:19:23.168 --> 00:19:25.652
- But, now we'll adjust.

00:19:25.652 --> 00:19:27.419
You know you get to adjust.

00:19:27.419 --> 00:19:29.205
You get to be a parent forever.

00:19:29.205 --> 00:19:33.172
All these parents want to
win in the first inning.

00:19:33.172 --> 00:19:34.005
There's more to it.

00:19:34.005 --> 00:19:35.743
Now I do think the early
years are foundational.

00:19:35.743 --> 00:19:37.348
I don't disrespect them.

00:19:37.348 --> 00:19:41.585
I do think feelings get
formulated at six, nine, 12,

00:19:41.585 --> 00:19:43.488
14, 19 that may be harder.

00:19:43.488 --> 00:19:44.975
But, let's be honest.

00:19:44.975 --> 00:19:46.080
I need people to understand.

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:47.883
I'm gonna paint the picture
that everybody knows

00:19:47.883 --> 00:19:50.402
is true and should really make all parents

00:19:50.402 --> 00:19:53.816
feel better about themselves
and not make them crippled.

00:19:53.816 --> 00:19:56.718
How many times do you need to see a child

00:19:56.718 --> 00:19:59.891
reunited with a parent
that did everything wrong,

00:19:59.891 --> 00:20:03.037
walked out on them, didn't
give a crap about them,

00:20:03.037 --> 00:20:05.665
cheated on their spouse, stole the money,

00:20:05.665 --> 00:20:08.150
alcoholic, drugs, it takes the kid

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:10.731
all but five minutes to
get one or two things

00:20:10.731 --> 00:20:12.976
off their chest, and
they're back into the need

00:20:12.976 --> 00:20:15.342
to rebuild that relationship.

00:20:15.342 --> 00:20:17.618
These are our children.

00:20:17.618 --> 00:20:18.562
- [Jim] It's baked in.

00:20:18.562 --> 00:20:20.025
